 > **Adopted revision 59 (throwing calibration; supersedes the earlier 0.7865 m and uncalibrated-size statements below):** Medium reference arm length **0.800 m**; lengths double per size category, acceleration path equals reference arm length, and the smooth empirical release-velocity equation with work-energy square-root-of-length scaling is normative. Equal Strength entails equal maximum lifting/carrying capacity regardless of size. The superseded revision 57–58 statements below are retained as chronological history, **not current rules**. See [Throwing](#throwing) and [Strength-based force capacity](#strength-based-force-capacity-calibration-evidence).
 > **Historical revision 58 (superseded by revision 59; ES-041 — Medium reference arm length):** The **Medium (3) Size Category** has a standard reference arm length of **0.7865 m (78.65 cm)**, the adult-male mean reported from the ANSUR II anthropometric survey. The measurement runs from the **acromion (shoulder tip) to the tip of the middle finger**, with the arm straight. This value is a standardized Medium-category geometry default, not an individual measurement, throwing acceleration path, or combat Reach; no values or scale factors have yet been assigned to the other seven categories. See [Strength-based force capacity: calibration evidence](#strength-based-force-capacity-calibration-evidence).
 > **Historical revision 57 (superseded by revision 59; ES-041 — size-category arm length):** For physical throwing-force calibration, take a Being’s **reference arm length from its Size Category**, using the existing [Size Categories Table](#size-categories-table) instead of defining a separate individual arm-length measurement. Standard reference lengths for the categories still require calibration; the table’s *effective size* values are not physical lengths or doubling rules. Arm length is a geometric input, **not automatically the object’s acceleration path**, and this choice does not change combat reach or set a Strength-to-newtons function. See [Strength-based force capacity: calibration evidence](#strength-based-force-capacity-calibration-evidence).
-> **Adopted revision 56 (ES-041; progressive-skill baseline and Throwing Table):** An **acquired progressive skill starts at level 1**; level 0 indicates no acquired level of that skill. Ordinary progressive skills can still be attempted untrained at level 0 where permitted; extraordinary prerequisites remain in force. For throwing, the rules give **+1 to the maximum-velocity formula when any Throw skill level is acquired** (not +1 for every level). Accordingly, the [Throwing Table](#throwing-table) assumes at least Throw level 1: at Strength 10, `5 × (10 + 1 − 2) / 2 = 22.5 yd/s`, matching the table. An untrained level-0 thrower at Strength 10 uses the formula with modifier 0, giving `20 yd/s` in otherwise identical conditions. This resolves the table/formula discrepancy without altering the weight/velocity data, and **does not** establish a newton force-capacity function. See [Throwing](#throwing), [Gaining Skill](#gaining-skill), and the [current decision record](encounters-consistency-decisions-r59.md).
-> **Adopted revision 55 (ES-041; maximum throwability versus effective range):** The **Max Weight** column of the [Throwing Table](#throwing-table) is the heaviest weight a Being of that Strength can **throw at all**, *not* the heaviest weight that can be thrown with sufficient speed to reach an adjacent hex. A thrown object below the 5 yd/s minimum for effective adjacent-hex throwing can still count as throwable, but its limited velocity is not sufficient for an effective throw into the adjacent hex. Maximum thrown weight, maximum launch speed (for favorable lighter loads), and maximum effective throwing weight are **different constraints**; do not assume table maxima apply simultaneously to the same object. The separate formula/table baseline was resolved in revision 56: the table assumes at least one Throw skill level (+1 velocity modifier). See [Throwing](#throwing), [Maximum weight](#maximum-weight), and the [current decision record](encounters-consistency-decisions-r59.md).
