 > **Adopted revision 47 (ES-041; Grab/Hold versus Grapple Escape):** **Break hold** is the action used to *break* an individual physical **Grab** or its continuation, **Hold**; existing voluntary release and failed-maintenance outcomes are unchanged. **Escape** is a Grapple-only action, available only in an established Grapple, and is resolved by the opposed Grapple check with the approved Escape Artist addition. Escape Artist does not add to Break hold and does not create a physical-grip Escape action. The skill retains its independent existing fixed-DC uses for ropes, entanglement, and confined spaces. The earlier revision-32 suggestion of Escape from a Grab is superseded. See [Break hold](#break-hold), [Grab](#grab), [Grapple](#grapple), and [Escape Artist](#escape-artist-ordinary-progressive-dexterity).
-> **Adopted revision 46 (ES-041; supersedes the continuing-grip prerequisite inferred in revision 45):** **Grab** establishes one physical grip; **Hold** maintains that Grab, and neither is itself **Grapple**. A successful Grab is necessary to *initiate* Grapple, but after Grapple is established, breaking any or all individual Holds **does not terminate the opponent’s Grapple**. An opponent’s established Grapple ends only through **Escape** or **unconsciousness**; a Being may independently end **their own Grapple action** by changing actions under the normal Multi-action constraints. The earlier revision-45 last-grip interruption rule is **superseded for an already-established Grapple** and now applies only to loss of a Grab *before a first Grapple check has established the action*. Give the Being whose unestablished attempt becomes ineligible the ordinary new-action choice. To keep the two concepts distinct, the **Grab check** is separately defined with the same opposed 3d6 + Improved Grapple + best applicable ability bonus + modifiers structure as the **Grapple check**, but it governs establishing/maintaining **individual Grabs/Holds**, not Grapple position transitions. This replaces the former d20-versus-d20 opposed Grab check for unwilling Beings; the initial touch attack, object-grabbing rules, and Break hold Strength contest remain distinct. The first Grapple check still replaces a coincident first Grab-maintenance check as previously adopted. See [Grab](#grab), [Grapple](#grapple), and the [current decision record](encounters-consistency-decisions-r59.md).
-> **Historical revision 45 (ES-041; limited/superseded by revision 46 for established Grapples):** When a previously established physical grip that is **necessary for a participant’s pending Grapple action** is actually released and no other qualifying grip remains, **terminate that participant’s not-yet-resolved Grapple action immediately when the release takes effect**. Cancel its pending check and preparation, and give that participant the ordinary opportunity to declare a new action at that same decision point, without completing the canceled Grapple or granting an extra instantaneous action. Releasing A’s grip does not cancel unrelated actions or a Grapple still supported by an independent qualifying grip. If the Break hold and Grapple check are due in the **same second**, resolve both against the common pre-action state before committing the release; no same-second retroactive nullification. This qualifies revision 44’s preservation of independently declared actions: **changing A’s action alone** cannot terminate B’s Grapple, but actually releasing B’s last required grip in an earlier second can. See [One-second action and defense resolution](#one-second-action-and-defense-resolution), [Grab](#grab), [Grapple](#grapple), and [current decision record](encounters-consistency-decisions-r59.md).
-> **Adopted revision 44 (ES-041):** A Grapple action belongs to the combatant who declared it. When A declares the one-second [Break hold](#break-hold) without an eligible [Multiple actions](#multiple-actions) declaration, **A terminates A's own Grapple action**, including its unfinished preparation, but **does not terminate, interrupt, or cancel B's independently declared Grapple action**. The change of action alone does not release any established physical Grab; each grip persists until an applicable release or maintenance outcome ends it. A successful Break hold releases only its targeted hold. This refines the action-specific meaning of “interrupt Grapple” in revision 43, without changing Multi-action eligibility, checks, or timing. See [Grab](#grab), [Grapple](#grapple), and the [current decision record](encounters-consistency-decisions-r59.md).
-> **Adopted revision 43 (ES-041):** A participant already grappling may attempt the one-second [Break hold](#break-hold) action **without interrupting Grapple only through a legally declared [Multiple actions](#multiple-actions) program**, which requires the Multi-action skill and follows its normal priority penalties, timing and compatibility limits. **Without Multi-action**, switching to Break hold **interrupts the declaring combatant’s own Grapple action** and cancels any pending preparation for that action; revision 44 makes explicit that another participant’s Grapple action continues. Break hold still contests 3d6 + Strength bonus and targets only one grip; no free extra action or automatic Grapple Escape is created. This qualifies the earlier general change-of-action statements (revisions 38–39). See [Grapple](#grapple) and the [current decision record](encounters-consistency-decisions-r59.md).
-> **Adopted revision 42 (ES-041):** An unsuccessful initial opposed Grapple check (including a tie) **does not end the successful prerequisite Grab**. The Grab remains established until a distinct event or applicable rule actually terminates that grip. In particular, when the initial Grapple check replaces a coincident Grab-maintenance check (revision 41), a failed Grapple transition does not retroactively create a failed Grab-maintenance result. The ordinary separately applicable Grab-maintenance, Break hold, and other release rules still apply. See [Grab](#grab), [Grapple](#grapple), and the [current decision record](encounters-consistency-decisions-r59.md).
-> **Adopted revision 41 (ES-041):** When the first opposed Grapple skill check after a successful Grab resolves at the same time that an opposed attack/defense Grab-maintenance check would otherwise be due **for that same grip**, resolve **only the Grapple check**. The Grapple check replaces the coincident Grab-maintenance check; no preliminary or additional maintenance roll is made for that grip at that moment. The Grapple check retains its ordinary six-second base preparation and valid Combat Strategy timing reductions, its existing dice formula, and its usual outcomes. Independently held grips are not covered by this replacement. See [Grab](#grab), [Grapple](#grapple), and the [current decision record](encounters-consistency-decisions-r59.md).
-> **Adopted revision 40 (ES-041):** Maintaining a Grab on an unwilling target requires a **Grab check every six seconds**, using the same **opposed attack/defense procedure** as establishing that Grab: the holder rolls an attack using their attack level; the held target rolls the defense-level comparison. The holder must obtain the higher modified result to retain that particular grip; on failure or a tie, the grip is released. This is not the one-second [Break hold](#break-hold) Strength contest or the six-second opposed [Grapple](#grapple) skill check. The later revision 41 settles coincident same-grip maintenance at the initial Grapple transition; multiple-hand grips and Escape eligibility remain open. See [Grab](#grab) and the [current decision record](encounters-consistency-decisions-r59.md).
-> **Revision 39 editorial reconciliation (ES-041):** The one-second action for breaking an individual Grab uses the **existing Break hold Strength contest**, rather than a Grab-style opposed attack/defense roll or the six-second opposed Grapple skill check. Each side rolls **3d6 + Strength bonus**; the breaker must achieve the higher total to release the single targeted hold, and a tie leaves that hold in place. The action-change requirement from revision 38 remains. No new roll mechanics have been introduced; see [Break hold](#break-hold), [Grab](#grab), and the [current decision record](encounters-consistency-decisions-r59.md).
-> **Adopted revision 38 (ES-041):** Breaking an individual Grab is an intentional **change of action**. A combatant must cancel any action still being prepared and not yet resolved in order to declare an attempt to break a Grab. The abandoned action does not later resolve; a completed action is unaffected. The new attempt follows the Grab section’s existing **one-second, one-hold-at-a-time** timing and the ordinary decision/timeline process. This does not create a free incidental Grab-break check or modify the distinct opposed Grapple timing. The separate Break hold Strength contest is cross-referenced in revision 39; Grab maintenance and multi-hand interactions remain under review. See [Grab](#grab) and the [current decision record](encounters-consistency-decisions-r59.md).
-> **Adopted revision 36–37 (ES-041):** Independently declared simultaneous Grapple actions that succeed but require incompatible resulting control states are rerolled against the same frozen state, with no time elapsing. For two participants, repeat until exactly one succeeds. For three or more, reroll every involved participant until at least one succeeds and every successful outcome is compatible; multiple compatible successes may commit together. No interim state is committed; unrelated simultaneous attacks and other compatible effects still resolve normally. See [Grapple](#grapple) and the [current decision record](encounters-consistency-decisions-r59.md).
-> **Adopted revision 35 (ES-041):** A completed **Grab** establishes the prerequisite for Grapple, but does **not** itself resolve a Grapple skill check. After the successful Grab becomes established, the first opposed Grapple check requires its ordinary **six-second preparation** (subject to permitted Combat Strategy timing reduction); it cannot occur instantaneously on Grab success. Keep the distinct Grab attack/defense check and its own timing. A legal earlier check selected by either grappling participant is shared by both, as adopted in revision 30. Competing same-second Grapple actions have since been resolved under the no-time conflict rule in revisions 36–37; other Grab-specific interactions remain open under ES-041. See [Grab](#grab), [Grapple](#grapple), and the [decision record](encounters-consistency-decisions-r59.md).
-> **Adopted revision 34 (ES-041):** Multiple combatants attempting to Grab, Hold, or Grapple the same target make **independent checks**, not a pooled group check. For each qualifying attacker, the existing held-opponent bonus is determined by the effective sizes of **other attackers already holding or grappling the target** and by the target’s effective size. Preserve existing size-capacity restrictions and use the appropriate Grab-versus-Grapple check type. Simultaneously initiated attempts do not confer new bonus eligibility on one another before the universal state commitment. The first opposed Grapple check after a successful Grab requires its ordinary six-second preparation (subject to legal Combat Strategy timing adjustments); incompatible same-second declarations have since been addressed in revisions 36–37. See [Multiple attackers](#grab), [Grapple](#grapple), and the [current decision record](encounters-consistency-decisions-r59.md).
-> **Adopted revision 33 (ES-041):** A valid declaration of **Takedown, Reverse, or Pin** is resolved by the **one opposed Grapple skill check** scheduled for that interval. This check simultaneously contests continuation of the current position and resolves the declared transition; it requires **no separate preliminary Hold-maintenance check** and no follow-up check for the action. On success, apply only the action’s existing position prerequisites, size limits, and consequences. On failure or tie, preserve the current position. Timing remains the earliest legally scheduled check selected by either participant. Grab-specific release, holding and multi-hand interactions remain open under ES-041; simultaneous incompatible successes were resolved in revisions 36–37. See [Grapple](#grapple), [Takedown/Reverse/Pin](#grapple), and [Revision 33 decision record](encounters-consistency-decisions-r59.md).
